{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1121520-tabletop-rpgs-dying","id":1121520,"title":"Tabletop RPGs Dying?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-07-27T14:06:41-07:00","id":1121520,"title":"Tabletop RPGs Dying?","content":"Just curious about this. A while ago the World of Darkness roleplaying line became a &quot;print on demand&quot; product. I believe other game lines are doing the same thing. In fact, at most of the gaming stores I visit (Denver) the only roleplaying games you can find are D&amp;D. Card Games and minatures games at these stores are HUGE and thus are given most of the shelf-space.<br /> <br /> With this in mind, is tabletop roleplaying a dying trend? I know there are certainly die-hard fans and players, but overall is tabletop roleplaying a dying trend?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13210-fossmano1","name":"FossMaNo1","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T14:12:28-07:00","id":1121524,"title":"","content":"I think the market is getting away from big name companies and into smaller &quot;indie&quot; developers. Since it's pretty easy to through together a pdf and put it up on drivethrurpg, you will still have your big names but just look at computer games and Steam.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13009-burritomage","name":"BurritoMage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13009_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"585"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T14:13:03-07:00","id":1121525,"title":"","content":"Quite the opposite; the indie RPG has absolutely exploded, to the point where we're kind of in a golden age. Gen Con is so so huge they can't fit everyone who wants to attend or sell there, and there's countless smaller cons as well. The Adventure Zone and Critical Role are both massively popular, paving the way for other podcasts and web shows. Self-publishing has never been easier, and I know some designers who supplement their income or live entirely off of Patreon revenue. There's multiple successful RPG crowdfunding campaigns ever single month.<br /> <br /> Now, are physical retail and physical books dying? Yup. But RPGs are thriving.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T14:14:20-07:00","id":1121528,"title":"","content":"no, i think its just changing from and industry that sells books and has moved along with so many other industries to digital distribution, sure the books are still available (on demand) but pdf is also available and its cheaper.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12845-ahather","name":"ahather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12845_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"238"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T14:17:18-07:00","id":1121532,"title":"","content":"No, quite the opposite.<br /> It's just that PDFs and tablets make it less necessary that a game is sold on shelves. You can get decent figures from 3D printer shops online and Indies are at record sales. It's the stores that are having the issue. People can now find tons of games online. You don't need to hang out there anymore, since you can find a group via websites and again: PDF is king. Wargames still need them, since tables and terrain are pretty unwieldy and tough to move. But RPGs are growing. <br /> Even My Little Pony has it's own RPG line these days! We have literally entered a golden age!<br /> (BTW: Tails of Equestria is a really good starter RPG)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13654-davesknd","name":"Davesknd","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"173"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T19:34:18-07:00","id":1121670,"title":"","content":"Tabletop RPGs are not dying, but it's harder to see the games at stores. When I started playing Mage: the Ascension in 1995, you had to go to a game store to get most games. There was mail order for a few publishers, but the internet was new and even graphical browsers were pretty weak. We didn't have a lot of PDFs. Though there were forums. . . Now, about 20 years later, well it's hard to get anything not in PDF.<br /> <br /> As a slice of the gaming pie, RPGs are pretty small. ICV isn't the only source of games sales, which are difficult to track as most of the publishers are small. However, ICV has 29% sales growth from 2015 to 2016. Still, $45 million out of $1.435 billion, the RPG's share of the hobby games market, is really small. To put the hobby games market in perspective, the hobby games market according to ICV, is about the same size as Hasbro's games net revenue. Hasbro reported $1.387 billion in net revenue from their games segment in 2016, up 9% from 2015. Some of that Hasbro sales will overlap with the games industry sales, since they own Magic &amp; Dungeons and Dragons. However, you also have to consider that Hasbro owns a ton of classic games such as Monopoly (with all those licenses), Clue, Life, Yahtzee and Taboo, which are sold to those outside the hobby more than inside the hobby.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://files.shareholder.com/downloads/HAS/4885507176x0x935515/842829F9-4911-4850-8770-B8106CFAA447/Hasbro_2016_Annual_Report_Final.pdf\" target=\"_blank\">http://files.shareholder.com/downloa...port_Final.pdf</a><br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://icv2.com/articles/news/view/38012/hobby-games-market-over-1-4-billion\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://icv2.com/images/article_thumbs/650x650_b2386bfa9d9d4b1f46e9a8a21ea5d2d704215b695fd986053f2688cf.jpg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://icv2.com/articles/news/view/38012/hobby-games-market-over-1-4-billion\" target=\"_blank\"> Hobby Games Market Over $1.4 Billion </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://icv2.com/articles/news/view/38012/hobby-games-market-over-1-4-billion\" target=\"_blank\"> https://icv2.com/articles/news/view/38012/hobby-games-market-over-1-4-billion </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> In 2016 </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Tabletop RPGs are doing well, but the margins are thin. With 3-D printing and PDFs, you need a game store less often than you used to. A physical game store, just like bookstores and other stores selling non-perishable goods that you can buy on Amazon, costs a lot to operate. So, the game stores that survive are doing so by being more than a place to just buy the game books.<br /> <br /> I'm not a computer game expert, but I haven't bought a computer game at a store in years. I'll buy a few on Steam when I get the itch to play a computer game. I buy RPGs direct from the publisher through preorders or Kickstarters or on drivethrurpg.com regularly. If I don't need a print copy, PDF is great and I have no storage issues.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13680-baakyocalder","name":"baakyocalder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13680_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"353"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T19:36:09-07:00","id":1121671,"title":"","content":"Oh, and while I prefer paper books and game stores, much of what I like isn't sold through the game stores. I'm not buying CCGs or D&amp;D, so except for miniatures and maps and dice, it's hard to get anything I want at a store. If I had a good 3D printer, I imagine I'd not need to go to a store.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13680-baakyocalder","name":"baakyocalder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13680_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"353"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T21:43:04-07:00","id":1121707,"title":"","content":"There are also some game publishers that are still printing their books, they just choose to sell them more directly to their customers instead of selling to distributors from which local book or game shops can order. So what is or isn't on the shelves of a local game shop isn't really a clear indicator one way or another about how healthy the hobby is.<br /> <br /> ...but if you go look at the numbers of people playing games through services like Roll20 (something like 73,000 players in the first quarter of this year, and that's up from the prior quarter), or how many gaming sessions get stream on Twitch or the like, you can see more clearly that the RPG hobby is doing quite well these days.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Not so noble anymore.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1823-drake","name":"Drake","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1823_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"456"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T21:43:41-07:00","id":1121709,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Davesknd</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1121520-tabletop-rpgs-dying?p=1121532#post1121532\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No, quite the opposite.<br /> It's just that PDFs and tablets make it less necessary that a game is sold on shelves. You can get decent figures from 3D printer shops online and Indies are at record sales. It's the stores that are having the issue. People can now find tons of games online. You don't need to hang out there anymore, since you can find a group via websites and again: PDF is king. Wargames still need them, since tables and terrain are pretty unwieldy and tough to move. But RPGs are growing.<br /> Even My Little Pony has it's own RPG line these days! We have literally entered a golden age!<br /> (BTW: Tails of Equestria is a really good starter RPG)</div> </div> </div> </div>I'll also point out that production quality seems to be going up (along with prices but eh). More art, better art, better layout all that goodness. I mean if you want scary layouts take a look at some of the published 3rd party D&amp;D 3.0 and 3.5 products, or ones before that.<br /> <br /> Game stores? Yeah they're dying. but so is much of retail, particularly non-differentiated speciality retail... if you're buying a box set or a book, it doesn't actually matter *where* said book comes from, it's all the same book no matter if you're getting it from a local shop or online... so people default to online because they have lower overhead. Same with a lot of retail shops these days.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T22:05:29-07:00","id":1121714,"title":"","content":"I'd expect more and more of an online presence with these things. Products like Hero Lab have been dominating the whole automated character sheet thing for years but making something similar is no longer beyond the ability for most companies now. I believe Dungeons and Dragons just put out a new app and putting the tabletop online is something they've been wanting to do since before fourth edition, but was derailed by an untimely murder suicide. And of course there's all the sites that have realized, hey, not everyone games at the table anymore, lets give them some online options.<br /> <br /> If you listen to some of the horror stories of the smaller RPG companies in trying to get their books into stores you can't really blame them for going the other way. Especially since it's not as if there's more and more bookstores opening up. My local gaming store and Barnes and Noble have the same size collection of gaming books, about one rack, with most of the game store being devoted to miniatures, board games, which seem 'in' right now, cards, and space for playing. Their content isn't that different, either, with mostly Dungeons and Dragons and Pathfinder and one or two products from a few smaller companies.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T22:36:48-07:00","id":1121719,"title":"","content":"Yeah, it's not RPGs that are dying but the physical print medium. Really the only reason you even see D&amp;D on store shelves any more is that Wizards of the Coast is afraid to fully embrace digital distribution. That, and they can afford to take the risk of losing money on physical printing, since they have such powerful brand recognition and make most of the money off non-book merchandise like minis, map tiles, DM screens, spell cards, branded board games, etc. Now they're even selling 10 blank character sheets in a nice folder for 10 bucks.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T22:43:21-07:00","id":1121723,"title":"","content":"Although, it's all fine and good and it's good to see that the hobby is doing well, but I miss the physicality and sheer joy of browsing in a game store, talking to like-minded people and all that stuff. Yes, ordering online and electronic formats are more convenient (or not, it depends), but it doesn't have the social hub and sheer tangibility aspects of stores. I might add it's not just about rpgs, but specialty music and clothing/attires shops too. The internet really did some wonderful things for various hobbies and subcultures, but in my eyes, it really took away a lot from other aspects and from the wonder.<br /> <br /> And I'm just 28...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T23:41:47-07:00","id":1121732,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1121520-tabletop-rpgs-dying?p=1121723#post1121723\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Although, it's all fine and good and it's good to see that the hobby is doing well, but I miss the physicality and sheer joy of browsing in a game store, talking to like-minded people and all that stuff. Yes, ordering online and electronic formats are more convenient (or not, it depends), but it doesn't have the social hub and sheer tangibility aspects of stores. I might add it's not just about rpgs, but specialty music and clothing/attires shops too. The internet really did some wonderful things for various hobbies and subcultures, but in my eyes, it really took away a lot from other aspects and from the wonder.<br /> <br /> And I'm just 28...</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree. I understand that it's easier and less expensive to shop on the internet than visit a store in person, and more expensive for a local store to stay in business than a big box internet store, but I don't like that the internet is driving the local stores *out* of business. I like to go walk among real shelves in person, where I can touch and see more than flat thumbnail images on a screen. Books are easier to buy sight unseen than many products, like cars or vegetables or shoes, where testing the product in person makes a big difference, so print media generally may really be a diminishing market for physical stores. Even when buying on the internet, I'd rather pay more for the real printed books with real pages than just get the pdf or e-book for screen reading. Perhaps I've just reached the age where I don't understand kids these days. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13713-wilson","name":"Wilson","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"182"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-28T00:05:43-07:00","id":1121735,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1121520-tabletop-rpgs-dying?p=1121723#post1121723\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Although, it's all fine and good and it's good to see that the hobby is doing well, but I miss the physicality and sheer joy of browsing in a game store, talking to like-minded people and all that stuff. Yes, ordering online and electronic formats are more convenient (or not, it depends), but it doesn't have the social hub and sheer tangibility aspects of stores. I might add it's not just about rpgs, but specialty music and clothing/attires shops too. The internet really did some wonderful things for various hobbies and subcultures, but in my eyes, it really took away a lot from other aspects and from the wonder.<br /> <br /> And I'm just 28...</div> </div> </div> </div>I've met a fuckton more of any social group I can think to name online then I ever would've physically.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-28T00:14:15-07:00","id":1121738,"title":"","content":"Everyone's experiences are going to be different. Even including the bad ones I've had a lot better circumstances gaming online than I did at the table, until I found my group.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-28T05:50:34-07:00","id":1121794,"title":"","content":"Not dying...just shambling along like a newly made zombie. Refusing to die...again.<br /> <br /> The vast majority of people who work in the industry have day jobs, however. RPG industry is not very lucrative.<br /> <br /> I feel like it's definitely now more a labor of love for many developers. Many current RPG writers were just plain ol' regular forum users and fans of the IP.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\">&quot;Steel isn't strong, boy. Flesh is stronger.&quot;</span></span></span>","edited":{"userid":95,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/95-fat-larry","user":"Fat Larry","date":". 07-28-2017, 09:30 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/95-fat-larry","name":"Fat Larry","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar95_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-28T08:38:31-07:00","id":1121873,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1121520-tabletop-rpgs-dying?p=1121719#post1121719\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah, it's not RPGs that are dying but the physical print medium. Really the only reason you even see D&amp;D on store shelves any more is that Wizards of the Coast is afraid to fully embrace digital distribution. That, and they can afford to take the risk of losing money on physical printing, since they have such powerful brand recognition and make most of the money off non-book merchandise like minis, map tiles, DM screens, spell cards, branded board games, etc. Now they're even selling 10 blank character sheets in a nice folder for 10 bucks.</div> </div> </div> </div>It doesn't hurt that WotC can afford to lose money for a long, long time, thanks to the financial ablative armor that is how much fucking money Magic: the Gathering brings in.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-28T12:11:59-07:00","id":1121990,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1121520-tabletop-rpgs-dying?p=1121723#post1121723\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Although, it's all fine and good and it's good to see that the hobby is doing well, but I miss the physicality and sheer joy of browsing in a game store, talking to like-minded people and all that stuff. Yes, ordering online and electronic formats are more convenient (or not, it depends), but it doesn't have the social hub and sheer tangibility aspects of stores. I might add it's not just about rpgs, but specialty music and clothing/attires shops too. The internet really did some wonderful things for various hobbies and subcultures, but in my eyes, it really took away a lot from other aspects and from the wonder.<br /> <br /> And I'm just 28...</div> </div> </div> </div>This is pretty much exactly the experience with every technology shift. There are good things that go away, there are bad things that go away, there are good things that start up and bad things that start up. the VCR was a boon to pornographers and niche market movie producers, and a massive blow to big ticket movie producers for example, resulting in calls about moral decay and consumer freedom.<br /> <br /> Ultimately what I'm getting at is that the only constant is change.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-28T12:40:00-07:00","id":1122006,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1121520-tabletop-rpgs-dying?p=1121735#post1121735\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I've met a fuckton more of any social group I can think to name online then I ever would've physically.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's a part of why I said the internet did wonderful things, but took away a lot too. It's a boon and a curse, in my eyes. Yes it makes finding groups (rpg, or any kind of social ones, or sport-related, or literally anything) a lot easier, even from the other side of the world. In the meantime, I don't think living the majority of one's social life on it is a positive thing. I'm saying that as someone, who did exactly that, in the past several years, due to a panic attacks. You could have people to talk to, even to play rpgs with on the internet, but, depending on where they're living, you can't take a walk with many of them, can't gather for a physical session, drink a beer, go out to buy some clothes together, going to a concert, etc.<br /> <br /> I might add, I like online gaming (I'm even a big supporter of it, trying to convince our group constantly to try it, because we could have more frequent sessions and try a lot more things, but they're resisting staunchly and I get it, gathering for a gaming night is just a special thing), because it gives a lot of options, however, I also think physicality is important, whether it's meeting with people personally, or a special space like-minded people would naturally gather (like a shop, club or bar, or anything else), or the simple joy of browsing physical books on the self. I remember the first time when I got into an rpg shop. It was (or is, as far as I know, it still stands) a very small, one-room bookshop, with rulebooks and novels on every shelf, up to the ceiling and even on the floor, miniatures, dices, cards, some board games. It was truly a magical place and it was also like home. From that on, and even today, I just like going into a shop and spend some time around. An online shop just doesn't offer that experience. Also, no, even the most smartly layouted pdf doesn't offer the same wow effect of a physical book.<br /> <br /> Also, one problem I have, with the hobby going online is that it puts even more distance between existing fans and potential ones. You could wander into a hobby store, see a sign about the weekly organized play on the front and try it on a whim. You could, back then pick a Vampire book from the shelf of a totally average bookstore, because the green marble and rose looked cool and you wondered what could be this vampire-thing? With pdfs and online shopping you have to know what you're seeking, to find it and it's a model which is catering a lot more to existing fans, which isn't the best thing long-term. I think that's why WotC wants to keep books in shops and support game stores and I think they're somewhat right.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-28T12:42:16-07:00","id":1122008,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1121520-tabletop-rpgs-dying?p=1121873#post1121873\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It doesn't hurt that WotC can afford to lose money for a long, long time, thanks to the financial ablative armor that is how much fucking money Magic: the Gathering brings in.</div> </div> </div> </div>I... don1t really think they're loosing money on 5e. At all. As far as I know it seems going very well. The PHB is in it's, I don't know, 4th print? 5th?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-28T12:50:40-07:00","id":1122014,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Odd_Canuck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1121520-tabletop-rpgs-dying?p=1121990#post1121990\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is pretty much exactly the experience with every technology shift. There are good things that go away, there are bad things that go away, there are good things that start up and bad things that start up. the VCR was a boon to pornographers and niche market movie producers, and a massive blow to big ticket movie producers for example, resulting in calls about moral decay and consumer freedom.<br /> <br /> Ultimately what I'm getting at is that the only constant is change.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, but the internet is a bigger change for how we're living our lives than many things were for a long, long time. I think it's certainly the biggest change in how we're living our social life and a lot of it isn't rainbow and sunshine in my eyes.<br /> <br /> Just to put it bluntly, I <i>like </i>the internet, I just don't like <i>some</i> of the social changes it made. Others, I quite like. For example, that big publishers are a lot less of a gatekeeper, due to things like Patreon and crowdfounding and social media is a very good thing in my eyes.<br /> <br /> Also, just because there's change (which is inevitable), it doesn't mean that all change is good, that we should just shrug at it passively or that there aren't things worth keeping, even if they're more inconvenient in some parts, because they're adding other positive factors to our lives and convenience isn't the be-all-end-all. Vinyl disks, anyone?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":6363,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","user":"PM\u00e1rk","date":". 07-28-2017, 04:00 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-28T12:57:29-07:00","id":1122018,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Fat Larry</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1121520-tabletop-rpgs-dying?p=1121794#post1121794\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not dying...just shambling along like a newly made zombie. Refusing to die...again.<br /> <br /> The vast majority of people who work in the industry have day jobs, however. RPG industry is not very lucrative.<br /> <br /> I feel like it's definitely now more a labor of love for many developers. Many current RPG writers were just plain ol' regular forum users and fans of the IP.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, that too. It's concerning to me. Back then, there were less games yes, and less indie creators, doing their stuff (which is frequently very specialized and quite niche and for that and publishing models, reaches a lot fewer people), but a lot more publishers with actual staff and writers and developers making rpgs as a day job.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-28T14:51:30-07:00","id":1122066,"title":"","content":"Well I know one company that's dying.....<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://www.polygon.com/2017/6/19/15812486/geek-chic-out-of-business-game-tables\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://static0.polygonimages.com/wordpress/wp-content/uploads/chorus/uploads/chorus_asset/file/8710387/vizier_4.png?w=1200&amp;h=675&amp;fit=crop\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.polygon.com/2017/6/19/15812486/geek-chic-out-of-business-game-tables\" target=\"_blank\"> Geek Chic, maker of exquisite gaming tables, has gone out of business </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.polygon.com/2017/6/19/15812486/geek-chic-out-of-business-game-tables\" target=\"_blank\"> https://www.polygon.com/2017/6/19/15812486/geek-chic-out-of-business-game-tables </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> Thousands in customer deposits stuck in limbo </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13009-burritomage","name":"BurritoMage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13009_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"585"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-28T14:55:38-07:00","id":1122069,"title":"","content":"People just don't appreciate finely made furniture as they should. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> All hail Ikea, I guess.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, it's one of the best examples I ever seen of a very specialized, very high-end (thus, not cheap) product, aiming at a market which generally doesn't have this kind of money for such luxuries. For me, I never felt the need for a special gaming table, I'm more like a couch/carpet/easy chair guy.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":6363,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","user":"PM\u00e1rk","date":". 07-28-2017, 05:59 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-28T15:04:11-07:00","id":1122073,"title":"","content":"I think a lot of people who might have that kind of thing are do-it-yourselfers. A friend of mine has a massive game table with whiteboard top and dice and book drawers and so forth that we made.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-28T15:06:58-07:00","id":1122078,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>BurritoMage</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1121520-tabletop-rpgs-dying?p=1122066#post1122066\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well I know one company that's dying.....<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://www.polygon.com/2017/6/19/15812486/geek-chic-out-of-business-game-tables\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.polygon.com/2017/6/19/15...ss-game-tables</a></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Wow, I can't imagine spending that much money on a gaming table. Of course I suppose some people have pool tables or ping pong tables in their home but still.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-28T15:12:05-07:00","id":1122083,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1121520-tabletop-rpgs-dying?p=1122078#post1122078\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Wow, I can't imagine spending that much money on a gaming table.</div> </div> </div> </div>While there are so much special thematic dices and leather bound deluxe editions of books, I thought instantly...<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-28T16:22:14-07:00","id":1122119,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1121520-tabletop-rpgs-dying?p=1122073#post1122073\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think a lot of people who might have that kind of thing are do-it-yourselfers. A friend of mine has a massive game table with whiteboard top and dice and book drawers and so forth that we made.</div> </div> </div> </div>Definitely. I've wanted a fancy gaming table for years now, but I'm absolutely building it myself - not just to save some money, but also so that it has the features I actually want and isn't burdening my wallet with features that I don't have a use for.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Not so noble anymore.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1823-drake","name":"Drake","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1823_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"456"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-29T10:19:35-07:00","id":1122385,"title":"","content":"The main problem with the geek chic tables isn't just their expense but their impracticality. All their features are pretty nifty... for doing one particular thing. They aren't very versatile on top of being bulky. Even the gamers I know who make six figures or more, including some with dedicated gaming rooms, haven't invested in one mostly because they don't have the space to devote to that kind of table. Not when a &quot;regular&quot; table with better dimensions will do just fine and can also be used for other things.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2507,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","user":"Caitiff Primogen","date":". 07-29-2017, 01:21 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","name":"Caitiff Primogen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2507_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-29T13:03:35-07:00","id":1122443,"title":"","content":"The price was always a factor for me. Back when I was a freshmen in college, my friends and I got a used 3-in-1 octagonal traditional gaming table (so flat, poker, bumper pool) for around $200. Sure, brand new they're closer to $1K these days, but that's what... half of a what Geek Chic was looking for at their low end?<br /> <br /> The octagonal table was more than big enough for having 6 people around. We figured out how to take the bumpers off,so we had a big felt lined empty surface for wargames (which we could cover and put games on hold). The poker side was our default RPG side, because each player had a well for dice or play aids (and a drink holder) leaving the middle with plenty of space for maps, minis, books to get stacked for reference, a music player for our GMs that liked to use that, and so on. Then we still had the flat side for meals, and casual/party games.<br /> <br /> It was bulky, sure, but I don't think you could have convinced us to pay more for something &quot;dedicated&quot; to RPGs when it meant paying way more for a slightly increase in features.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-29T13:47:23-07:00","id":1122467,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1121520-tabletop-rpgs-dying?p=1121525#post1121525\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Quite the opposite; the indie RPG has absolutely exploded, to the point where we're kind of in a golden age. Gen Con is so so huge they can't fit everyone who wants to attend or sell there, and there's countless smaller cons as well. The Adventure Zone and Critical Role are both massively popular, paving the way for other podcasts and web shows. Self-publishing has never been easier, and I know some designers who supplement their income or live entirely off of Patreon revenue. There's multiple successful RPG crowdfunding campaigns ever single month.<br /> <br /> Now, are physical retail and physical books dying? Yup. But RPGs are thriving.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm curious, how do people even find these? Social media? Seems a bit random.<br /> <br /> Like, the reason I'm even reading this information is because I was a longtime fan, knew that Onyx Path had the rights to this RPG, came to their forum and clicked their thread.<br /> Suppose I'M just a random normie. How do I learn that there are people playing tah-bel-top arh-pee-gees? How do I find a group? How do I find indie publishers? How do I find out about events?<br /> Yes, word-of-mouth, but you can't just rely on that. Without people tumbling upon these things in shops, and there apparently being no central hub website for RPG fans (or a number of popular mini RPG news sites), what is my entryway as a complete noob? I can't quite wrap my head around how a fan today would work.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2330-vree","name":"Vree","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"176"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-29T14:10:21-07:00","id":1122475,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vree</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1121520-tabletop-rpgs-dying?p=1122467#post1122467\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes, word-of-mouth, but you can't just rely on that. Without people tumbling upon these things in shops, and there apparently being no central hub website for RPG fans (or a number of popular mini RPG news sites), what is my entryway as a complete noob? I can't quite wrap my head around how a fan today would work.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's the same way it does in real life. Either you wander around looking for an interesting store to browse and find an RPG shop, you have an interest and seek it out directly through a phone book or other real world alternative to Google, or you hear about it from friends online.<br /> <br /> There's a lot of RPG news sites, and hubs for RPG fans to get together. Just plug 'rpg news sites' into google.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-29T15:12:44-07:00","id":1122513,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vree</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1121520-tabletop-rpgs-dying?p=1122467#post1122467\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm curious, how do people even find these? Social media? Seems a bit random.<br /> <br /> Like, the reason I'm even reading this information is because I was a longtime fan, knew that Onyx Path had the rights to this RPG, came to their forum and clicked their thread.<br /> Suppose I'M just a random normie. How do I learn that there are people playing tah-bel-top arh-pee-gees? How do I find a group? How do I find indie publishers? How do I find out about events?<br /> Yes, word-of-mouth, but you can't just rely on that. Without people tumbling upon these things in shops, and there apparently being no central hub website for RPG fans (or a number of popular mini RPG news sites), what is my entryway as a complete noob? I can't quite wrap my head around how a fan today would work.</div> </div> </div> </div>Exactly. And that's why most people start out this hobby with D&amp;D and now Pathfinder and WoD a while back (well and some other factors, like brand recognition). They are/were in the bookshops, in physical format. They had novels in them too, with advertisements of the rpg in the back (accidently, my first encounter with WoD was a Vampire novel in the local library). They have regular organized events, WoD has/had actively advertised LARP games on campuses, video games are using the IPs, etc. They are just approachable, while indie games are more catering to &quot;people in the know&quot;.<br /> <br /> So, I don't think TT rpgs are in trouble, in general, but it's because the big ones are successful in bringing in a lot of people, not because of the myriad niche games. They are good to have and needed, because eventually most people will want to branch out and try other things, not just play D&amp;D to the grave, but the big ones are keeping the hobby going and healthy.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":6363,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","user":"PM\u00e1rk","date":". 07-29-2017, 06:18 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-29T15:27:32-07:00","id":1122517,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vree</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1121520-tabletop-rpgs-dying?p=1122467#post1122467\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Suppose I'M just a random normie. How do I learn that there are people playing tah-bel-top arh-pee-gees? How do I find a group? How do I find indie publishers? How do I find out about events?</div> </div> </div> </div>I have one friend who relied entirely on physical game stores for his news about RPGs. He used to play with us and then moved to Charleston. A couple of years after Return of the Scarlet Empress came out, we were talking and he said how sad it was that Exalted was over and nothing new was coming out. I asked if he had picked up the Autochthonia book or Glories of the Most High or Masters of Jade. He had no idea what I was talking about. For him, if it wasn't being sold as a physical copy in a bookstore, it might as well not even have existed, and his local FLGS didn't have any of those books in print so he had no idea.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, I have three players in my current group who were introduced to RPGs via the internet. One of them first got interested in RPGs via the online comic Penny Arcade. She liked video games and would go there for the funny comics and then saw some talking about RPGs and slowly began getting more interested in roleplaying.<br /> <br /> Two others got into roleplaying via 4chan. One would frequent the automobile board while the other would frequent the LGBT board. In both cases, sometimes threads would pop up where people would begin discussing roleplaying games, or posting screenshots of funny stuff from the roleplaying board. Eventually they decided to drop by the RPG board, started lurking there and eventually got into wanting to play tabletop RPGs.<br /> <br /> So I think those sorts of places, websites which allow roleplaying games to dovetail or intersect with other hobbies will end up playing a big role in introduce new people to the RPG community in the future. And &quot;dinosaurs&quot; like my buddy who only get their RPG news from their FLGS are probably going to end up either having to catch up with the new era or go extinct and drop out of the hobby.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-29T15:30:53-07:00","id":1122520,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vree</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1121520-tabletop-rpgs-dying?p=1122467#post1122467\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm curious, how do people even find these? Social media? Seems a bit random.<br /> <br /> Like, the reason I'm even reading this information is because I was a longtime fan, knew that Onyx Path had the rights to this RPG, came to their forum and clicked their thread.<br /> Suppose I'M just a random normie. How do I learn that there are people playing tah-bel-top arh-pee-gees? How do I find a group? How do I find indie publishers? How do I find out about events?<br /> Yes, word-of-mouth, but you can't just rely on that. Without people tumbling upon these things in shops, and there apparently being no central hub website for RPG fans (or a number of popular mini RPG news sites), what is my entryway as a complete noob? I can't quite wrap my head around how a fan today would work.</div> </div> </div> </div>My current gaming group is the result of me putting out a call to my followers on Tumblr, asking if anyone would be interested in trying out tabletop gaming; as a result, I got ten trans women, the majority of whome are new to the hobby.<br /> <br /> There's also several very popular actual play podcasts, like I mentioned.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-29T18:23:50-07:00","id":1122557,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vree</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1121520-tabletop-rpgs-dying?p=1122467#post1122467\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm curious, how do people even find these? Social media? Seems a bit random.<br /> <br /> Like, the reason I'm even reading this information is because I was a longtime fan, knew that Onyx Path had the rights to this RPG, came to their forum and clicked their thread.<br /> Suppose I'M just a random normie. How do I learn that there are people playing tah-bel-top arh-pee-gees? How do I find a group? How do I find indie publishers? How do I find out about events?<br /> Yes, word-of-mouth, but you can't just rely on that. Without people tumbling upon these things in shops, and there apparently being no central hub website for RPG fans (or a number of popular mini RPG news sites), what is my entryway as a complete noob? I can't quite wrap my head around how a fan today would work.</div> </div> </div> </div>I got into the hobby as a complete noob the complete noob way. I went to meetup.com, typed in D&amp;D (because while I knew other games existed that was the one that was big) and found a local meetup. From there a couple months later I had met the people that would form the core of my gaming group.<br /> <br /> To find out about indy games and/or get games outside of Pathfinder/WotC drivethrurpg.com... which pretty much anyone on the internet talking about non D&amp;D-esk games will direct you to.<br /> <br /> Alternatively doing a search for &quot;where to meet tabletop gamers&quot; brings up several ways to encounter them.<br /> <br /> Alternatively alternatively, there's the simple old fashioned way of searching for a local convention for whatever it is you're interested in, going and seeing if you're interested. This applies to pretty much everything but it works well with gaming, and gives you a broad exposure to the local gaming peoples.<br /> <br /> <br /> Honestly, this is one of the arguments on the general topic of death of the game store that I find a little unconvincing, the &quot;but where will gamers find each other?&quot; argument. Now I've known a couple game stores that actually had playspaces for RPGs. And they work great for a place to meet people, much better than meeting in someones home in a lot of cases, particularly if you have a lot of people coming from different directions... but, those are not and never have been a profit center for the game stores. I've lot count of the number of times I've been in one and pretty much been driven out by the wargamers (and the accompanying noise) or more commonly the CCG players (which ARE a profit center for the store). However as far as actually making it easier for gamers to encounter each other in the first place? Not so much. I've seen a couple corkboards of Looking For Players or Looking For Group, but again they're far more common online these days. What purpose they has has VERY much been eclipsed by the internet. You want a basic overview of something? You google it, you don't do what you had to do 35 years ago and drive to the local library, hope to find a book or three on the topic and read them. You want to meet people of a particular interest? You use the internet, you don't drive to a store and hope to run into them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-29T19:45:02-07:00","id":1122573,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Odd_Canuck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1121520-tabletop-rpgs-dying?p=1122557#post1122557\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I got into the hobby as a complete noob the complete noob way. I went to meetup.com, typed in D&amp;D (because while I knew other games existed that was the one that was big) and found a local meetup. From there a couple months later I had met the people that would form the core of my gaming group.<br /> <br /> To find out about indy games and/or get games outside of Pathfinder/WotC drivethrurpg.com... which pretty much anyone on the internet talking about non D&amp;D-esk games will direct you to.<br /> <br /> Alternatively doing a search for &quot;where to meet tabletop gamers&quot; brings up several ways to encounter them.<br /> <br /> Alternatively alternatively, there's the simple old fashioned way of searching for a local convention for whatever it is you're interested in, going and seeing if you're interested. This applies to pretty much everything but it works well with gaming, and gives you a broad exposure to the local gaming peoples.<br /> <br /> <br /> Honestly, this is one of the arguments on the general topic of death of the game store that I find a little unconvincing, the &quot;but where will gamers find each other?&quot; argument. Now I've known a couple game stores that actually had playspaces for RPGs. And they work great for a place to meet people, much better than meeting in someones home in a lot of cases, particularly if you have a lot of people coming from different directions... but, those are not and never have been a profit center for the game stores. I've lot count of the number of times I've been in one and pretty much been driven out by the wargamers (and the accompanying noise) or more commonly the CCG players (which ARE a profit center for the store). However as far as actually making it easier for gamers to encounter each other in the first place? Not so much. I've seen a couple corkboards of Looking For Players or Looking For Group, but again they're far more common online these days. What purpose they has has VERY much been eclipsed by the internet. You want a basic overview of something? You google it, you don't do what you had to do 35 years ago and drive to the local library, hope to find a book or three on the topic and read them. You want to meet people of a particular interest? You use the internet, you don't drive to a store and hope to run into them.</div> </div> </div> </div>Those are good points, but I feel like it's missing something. Namely, that your experience was from a person, who was already interested in gaming and knew about D&amp;D and thus searched for a meetup. Sure, in this day and age a lot of people are encountering with the existence of rpgs, through sheer saturation, especially in the &quot;geek/nerd community&quot;, but still there are plenty of people out there who never heard about them at all. To reach them it's good to have other channels, like books in average bookshops, advertised events, non-gaming but related stuff, like novels and shops with enticing fronts. That's all I'm saying. Yes, shops could be important social places too (though that's mainly important, again, to existing fans), but that's only one aspect. Honestly, I never viewed them as places for actual playing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-29T20:22:16-07:00","id":1122581,"title":"","content":"D&amp;D is a household name and property. It's regularly referenced in TV and media, with several movies made. It's not like it's the 80's where it's a corner-case thing only the most isolated of people know about it. It may be mocked, right along with LARPing, but people know what it is in broad strokes, and for those that are even the slightest bit curious about what &quot;That D&amp;D thing&quot; is actually like... there's whole games on youtube that deal with it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-29T20:32:20-07:00","id":1122586,"title":"","content":"Ok, it could be a local cultural thing in this case. Here in Hungary, I must explain, even for young people what rpgs are and most of them didn't hear about even D&amp;D. It's more likely they'd know about Drizzt, or the Dragonlance books than D&amp;D itself. I don't know how is it in the US or other Western countries, but I won't be surprised if asking 20 random people on the street, even i major cities, you'd get 1 or 2 who knows about it.<br /> <br /> I think that'll change with the movie, if it will be a success. Like the Song of Ice and Fire books. They were big in fantasy circles, well-regarded, had a big fanbase, a CCG, roleplaying game, board game,but most people hadn't had a clue about them. Then, there was the show and BAM!, you could suddenly seen at least three person on any random public transport vehicle reading them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-30T07:05:50-07:00","id":1122694,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1121520-tabletop-rpgs-dying?p=1122586#post1122586\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ok, it could be a local cultural thing in this case. Here in Hungary, I must explain, even for young people what rpgs are and most of them didn't hear about even D&amp;D. It's more likely they'd know about Drizzt, or the Dragonlance books than D&amp;D itself. I don't know how is it in the US or other Western countries, but I won't be surprised if asking 20 random people on the street, even i major cities, you'd get 1 or 2 who knows about it.<br /> <br /> I think that'll change with the movie, if it will be a success. Like the Song of Ice and Fire books. They were big in fantasy circles, well-regarded, had a big fanbase, a CCG, roleplaying game, board game,but most people hadn't had a clue about them. Then, there was the show and BAM!, you could suddenly seen at least three person on any random public transport vehicle reading them.</div> </div> </div> </div>Huh. Here basically everyone has heard of D&amp;D. A lot of people don't really understand what it actually <i>is</i>, beyond some kind of nerdy fantasy game. But it's hard not to have some vague awareness of it when it's talked about in a significant number of popular TV shows.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-30T09:34:17-07:00","id":1122725,"title":"","content":"I recently played a drop-in D&amp;D game at a local game store where at least three of the participants, including the DM, were under 18. While this made me feel incredibly old I was also glad to see that even kids who have grown up with current gen vidya games and mobile dervices still see an attraction in unplugged pen-and-paper roleplaying.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","name":"Caitiff Primogen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2507_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-30T13:30:14-07:00","id":1122790,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1121520-tabletop-rpgs-dying?p=1122694#post1122694\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Huh. Here basically everyone has heard of D&amp;D. A lot of people don't really understand what it actually <i>is</i>, beyond some kind of nerdy fantasy game. But it's hard not to have some vague awareness of it when it's talked about in a significant number of popular TV shows.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ok, it seems like really a localized cultural phenomenon then (in both of our cases). I could come up with two reasons:<br /> <br /> 1. We're just more distanced from American pop-culture, even today, so the average population don't really aware of the more obscure points. Or even the not-so-obscure ones. Netflix, for example, isn't really a thing there and I bet the majority never heard about Deadpool before the movie, things like that.<br /> <br /> 2. D&amp;D after all and rpgs in general were started in the US and some of them were really big and a lot of people encountered with them one way or the other. Here, not so. They're well-known in some demographic circles, but others are completely unaware of them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Caitiff Primogen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1121520-tabletop-rpgs-dying?p=1122725#post1122725\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I recently played a drop-in D&amp;D game at a local game store where at least three of the participants, including the DM, were under 18. While this made me feel incredibly old I was also glad to see that even kids who have grown up with current gen vidya games and mobile dervices still see an attraction in unplugged pen-and-paper roleplaying.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, it was really good to see younger people, not only 25+ ones on the biggest regular D&amp;D event we have there too.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}